<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Breda</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Breda is a city in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-brabant">North Brabant</a> in the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. From 1403 it belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-nassau">House of Nassau</a>, the family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-silent">William the Silent</a>, and its castle was a seat of the princes of Orange. A key fortress in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-revolt">Dutch Revolt</a>, it was taken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurice-prince-of-orange">Maurice of Nassau</a> in 1590, recaptured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1625 and won back by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> in 1637, and the Treaty of Breda was signed there in 1667.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/breda.jpg" alt="Breda" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: M.Minderhoud · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Breda_kasteel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Peace of Münster in 1648 confirmed the division: the north, around 's-Hertogenbosch and <b>Breda</b>, became States Brabant, a territory ruled directly by the States General without a voice of its own, while the south remained under Spain and from 1714 under Austria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brabant">Duchy of Brabant · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Maurice of Nassau, stadtholder from 1585, and his cousin William Louis reformed the army with regular pay, smaller units, constant drill and volley fire, and with the engineer Simon Stevin they recaptured towns such as <b>Breda</b> in 1590 and won the Battle of Nieuwpoort in 1600.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Thirty Years’ War Spanish troops took <b>Breda</b> in 1625 and shared in the victory at Nördlingen in 1634, but the French defeated the Army of Flanders at the Battle of Rocroi in 1643, and Portugal won its war of independence at Montes Claros in 1665.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Treaty of <b>Breda</b> of 1667 ended the Second Anglo-Dutch War; the Dutch did not press their claim, keeping Suriname and the nutmeg island of Run instead.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-netherland">New Netherland · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Count Engelbert I acquired <b>Breda</b> through marriage in 1403, linking the family to the Netherlands.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-nassau-dillenburg">County of Nassau-Dillenburg</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1789 exiles organised an army at <b>Breda</b>, defeated the Austrians at Turnhout in October, and by December drove them from most of the country.</blockquote><a href="/state/united-belgian-states">United Belgian States</a></li></ul></section>
